* Encrypt a file with a prompted passphrase
Start with an existing `secret.txt`. OpenSSL prompts for the encryption passphrase, keeping it out of the command line.
@code bash
umask 077
openssl enc -aes-256-cbc -md sha512 -pbkdf2 -iter 100000 -salt -a -in secret.txt -out secret.enc
@end

* Decrypt with the same parameters
@code bash
openssl enc -d -aes-256-cbc -md sha512 -pbkdf2 -iter 100000 -a -in secret.enc -out decrypted.txt
@end
Keep the digest and iteration count with the recipe: they must match during decryption. Protect the plaintext input and output too.
CBC encryption does not authenticate the ciphertext; use this only with trusted encrypted input.

* Supply a passphrase to a script without a literal argument
Replace the path with a separately provisioned file readable only by the intended user.
@code bash
openssl enc -d -aes-256-cbc -md sha512 -pbkdf2 -iter 100000 -a -in secret.enc -out decrypted.txt -pass fd:3 3< /path/to/passphrase
@end
The passphrase is read from the first line. Storing it next to the ciphertext with the same access permissions does not protect against someone reading both.

Related: {:encrypt-using-openssl:}[public-key encryption]. Reference: {https://docs.openssl.org/3.0/man1/openssl-enc/}[openssl enc].
